         <dc:title>
            <rdf:Alt>
               <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">You are assisting a qualified digital forensic examiner by reviewing the attached digital forensic materials. Conduct an independent, evidence-led examination of the materials you can actually access. There is no assumed incident, suspect, or expected finding. Do not assume that the system was compromised or that any activity was malicious. Consider benign, administrative, experimental, and other explanations where appropriate. Do not infer a personâ€™s intent or identity without evidence. Use only the attached materials for case-specific conclusions. Do not rely on prior conversations or assume facts that are not present in the attachments. Do not execute files or follow links. First, inventory the attachments and state which files you were able to examine. If an archive, file, worksheet, record, or data type cannot be opened or examined, identify it and explain the limitation. Do not imply that you examined all evidence unless you did. For each relevant finding Separate the direct observation from your interpretation. Cite its precise source attachment name and internal path, plus the worksheet and row, record identifier, or other location where available. State what the evidence supports and what it does not establish. Give a confidence level (high, moderate, or low) and explain the main reason for it. Note relevant alternative explanations, contradictions, and missing corroboration. When reconstructing events, provide a timeline and identify the time source and timezone. Do not treat events occurring close together as proof that one caused another. Distinguish file presence, download, execution, configuration change, and user action where the evidence permits. Structure your response as follows Scope and evidence examined Executive assessment Findings, with source references and confidence Chronological timeline Correlations, contradictions, and alternative explanations Limitations and questions the evidence cannot answer Prioritized next examination steps Be concise where evidence is straightforward, but explain important reasoning. Do not invent artifacts, source references, timestamps, hash values, tool results, or certainty. If the evidence is insufficient to answer something, say so explicitly.</rdf:li>
            </rdf:Alt>
         </dc:title>
         <dc:format>application/pdf</dc:format>
